; ======================================================================
;  memmap_q.pi4 - Arduino UNO Q (QCM2290) memory-map constants.
;
;  This is the Q's answer to RaspberryPi4/Board/memmap.pi4. It is a
;  Q-LOCAL file: it supplies the named constants the PORTABLE core libs
;  (Anvil/Core/crc.pbi, Anvil/Core/memrange.pbi) reference, but with the
;  QCM2290's real numbers instead of the BCM2711's. Nothing here touches
;  a Pi-4 file - the core files reach these names by inclusion order in
;  anvilqcoreproof.pi4, exactly as board.pi4 reaches memmap.pi4's.
;
;  EVERY NUMBER BELOW IS A CONFIRMED SILICON FACT from the vault
;  (Arduino UNO Q port plan - phase 1.md, "ROOT-LEVEL CONFIRMATIONS
;  2026-08-29"), read off the running board over ADB from /proc/iomem and
;  /proc/device-tree - not an estimate:
;
;    DRAM base 0x40000000, three banks
;      0x40000000 + 0x3DA00000   (986 MB)
;      0x80000000 + 0x40000000   (1 GB)
;      0xC0000000 + 0x80000000   (2 GB)   -> top = 0x140000000  (~3.96 GB)
;    Large CONTIGUOUS free block 0x63900000-0x7D9FFFFF (~356 MB), clear of
;      the Qualcomm TZ/firmware/modem reserved apertures.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  #CRC_POLY - the IEEE 802.3 reflected polynomial.
;
;  Anvil/Core/crc.pbi references #CRC_POLY and does NOT define it; on the
;  Pi 4 the definition lives in Anvil/Core/state.pbi:101. state.pi4 also
;  carries the whole console/line-editor global set, none of which this
;  phase-1 proof uses, so it is NOT included here. The polynomial is a
;  public constant of the algorithm (the same value state.pi4 defines),
;  so restating it is not a fork of the core file - crc.pi4 is included
;  read-only and unchanged.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#CRC_POLY  = $EDB88320         ; IEEE 802.3, reflected

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DRAM extent - the Q's identity, reported by HwInfo.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#Q_DRAM_BASE     = $40000000       ; low bank base (confirmed)
#Q_DRAM_TOP      = $140000000      ; one past the top of the 2 GB bank
#Q_FREE_LO       = $63900000       ; confirmed contiguous free block, lo
#Q_FREE_HI       = $7D9FFFFF       ; confirmed contiguous free block, hi

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Payload windows - what Anvil/Core/memrange.pbi's InPayload() checks.
;
;  On the Pi 4 these are the two DRAM windows a loaded image may live in.
;  On the Q, a UEFI application's buffers are placed by the firmware
;  anywhere in DRAM, so the windows are set to span the WHOLE confirmed
;  DRAM extent (0x40000000 .. 0x13FFFFFFF). The consequence is a property
;  the host can predict without knowing where UEFI put the image: any
;  buffer that lives in the Q's DRAM classifies as "in a payload window",
;  so InPayload() over the proof buffer must return 1.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The monitor's stack top. On the Pi 4 (RaspberryPi4/Board/memmap.pi4)
;  Main() moves SP here by hand because the firmware's boot stack is in
;  the way. On the Q the firmware is UEFI and it hands us a perfectly good
;  stack that we must NOT move - the image returns into the boot manager
;  and UEFI's own stack has to still be there. So this is REPORTED, never
;  installed, and the value is the link-time stack top the build passes
;  with --stack-addr.
;
;  Moved here from qstubs_q.pi4 on 2026-09-08: it is a memory-map
;  constant, HwMonStack() is part of the map seam the core asks through,
;  and the two proof harnesses include this file and not that one.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#MON_STACK = $68000000

#PAY0_LO   = $40000000         ; low window: all of the 32-bit DRAM space
#PAY0_HI   = $FFFFFFFF
#PAY1_LO   = $100000000        ; high window: the DRAM tail above 4 GB
#PAY1_HI   = $13FFFFFFF

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Nominal "monitor" windows - what memrange.pi4's HitsMonitor() refuses.
;
;  There is no resident Anvil monitor on the Q in phase 1 (this is a
;  run-and-report EFI app, not the interactive monitor), so these name a
;  1 MB reserved-by-convention region INSIDE the confirmed free block.
;  They exist so the proof can exercise HitsMonitor() the way the real
;  monitor does and get a host-predictable answer: HitsMonitor() over the
;  monitor window itself must return non-zero, and over the proof buffer
;  (which UEFI places elsewhere) must return zero.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#MON_LO      = $63900000       ; nominal monitor code window (in free block)
#MON_HI      = $639FFFFF
#MON_DATA_LO = $63A00000       ; nominal monitor data window
#MON_DATA_HI = $63AFFFFF

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwMonLo() / HwMonHi() ARE NOT HERE. They belong with the memory map by
;  subject and they cannot live here by dependency: on this board the
;  answer comes from EFI_LOADED_IMAGE_PROTOCOL, and this file is included
;  BEFORE ArduinoQ/Board/qefi_q.unoq, which is what talks to the firmware.
;  They are at the foot of ArduinoQ/Board/qstubs_q.unoq instead, with the
;  reasoning. See the note on #MON_LO above for why the constants in this
;  file are not the answer.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
